examples/kaboom-crew/src/systems/warp-hole-system.ts (post fix #184):
  const recentlyWarpedTo = new Map<EntityId, { gx: number; gz: number }>();
  ...
  for (const [entityId, target] of recentlyWarpedTo) {
    if (!world.hasEntity(entityId)) { recentlyWarpedTo.delete(entityId); continue; }
    const pos = world.getComponent<GridPos>(entityId, GRID_POSITION);
    if (pos === undefined || pos.gx !== target.gx || pos.gz !== target.gz) {
      recentlyWarpedTo.delete(entityId);
    }
  }

Fix outlines:
  1. **Time-based grace window**: stamp `(entityId → { destPos, expiresAt })` and skip ALL warp cells for the entity until expiresAt. ~0.5 s = 2 cells at speed=4. Lives in the system closure, no schema change.
  2. **Pass-through design**: teleport delivers the entity to the cell ADJACENT to the destination in the direction they were walking (or to a deterministic non-warp non-blocked neighbour when direction is zero). The bomber is never STANDING on the destination — it's purely a portal. Eliminates the 'sticky destination' problem entirely. Slightly larger code change but more idiomatic.
  3. **Entity-wide cooldown**: stamp `(entityId → expiresAt)` independent of cell. Until expiresAt, ALL warp cells skip this entity. Simplest possible patch on top of fix #184.

Variant 2 matches the user's mental model best ('the warp is a door, I step through, not on it'). Variant 3 is the smallest diff if the dev wants to ship today.

Additional finding (worth its own ticket): `kaboom.gotoCell(2, 1)` throws `Cannot set component "AgentGoto" on missing entity "2"`. The agent-goto-system appears to use the cell COORDINATE as an entity id.
